<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Central Europe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> is the region between Western and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, broadly including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, the Czech lands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a> its western parts lay within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>. In the modern era much of it was ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a> dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> basin until 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/central-europe.jpg" alt="Central Europe" width="320" height="332" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NordNordWest; derivative work by Dch · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Grossgliederung_Europas-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> has no agreed boundaries, and which countries belong to it often depends on the nationality and historical outlook of the author. The concept appeared in the 19th century, when it was understood as a contact zone between the southern and northern areas of Europe and later between the eastern and western. Geography fixes its borders most clearly to the north, across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a>, and to the south, across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soca">Soča</a>–Krka–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sava">Sava</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> line, while the borders with Western and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> are less defined.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After Roman rule fell in the 5th century, Germanic tribes, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, Alemans and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a>, conquered parts of the west, while Slavic tribes lived mainly in the east. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> was dissolved around 1800, power was consolidated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">Hohenzollerns</a>, who cooperated at times but more often competed. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt-parliament">Frankfurt Parliament</a> of 1848 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> proposed merging the smaller German-speaking states with the multi-ethnic Habsburg monarchy, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and others opposed. The idea of Mitteleuropa later became popular in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a>, founded in 1871.</p><p>After 1918 the concept shifted to the east and the countries that had reappeared on the map. Central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> became a field for integration movements, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/little-entente">Little Entente</a>, formed in 1921 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>, and the Intermarium idea, neither of which succeeded. In the Second World War Nazi Germany occupied most of the region, and the mass murder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> emptied settlements that had existed for centuries. After the war much of it came under Soviet control, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-curtain">Iron Curtain</a> divided it, although <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> declared neutrality.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The states most often counted as Central European are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, Liechtenstein, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenia">Slovenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and many sources add <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. The region is mostly Catholic or historically both Catholic and Protestant. The first university east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a> was founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in 1348 by Charles IV, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Central Europe” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cutting its economic ties contributed to the prolonged economic disruption of the 1920s across <b>Central Europe</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Charlemagne conquered much of Western and <b>Central Europe</b> in a reign of nearly half a century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It was the only democracy in <b>Central Europe</b> to last until 1938, but centralism angered Slovak autonomists such as Andrej Hlinka, and the Sudeten German Party of Konrad Henlein won more votes than any other party in 1935.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The German Confederation was the framework of <b>Central Europe</b> between Napoleon and Bismarck: a league of sovereign states that kept the peace and repressed liberalism for half a century, saw the national revolution of 1848, and was destroyed by the Austro-Prussian rivalry that decided how Germany would be…</blockquote><a href="/empire/german-confederation">German Confederation · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its central problem, how to govern many peoples with distinct laws and languages under one dynasty, passed to the Austrian Empire and Austria-Hungary, and still informs debates about federalism and nationality in <b>Central Europe</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-monarchy">Habsburg Monarchy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The strikes at Kutná Hora made Bohemia one of the largest producers of silver in Europe, and around 1300 Wenceslaus II introduced the Prague groschen, a heavy silver coin minted at Kutná Hora that circulated across <b>Central Europe</b>, and issued a mining code, the Ius regale montanorum.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
